<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">18 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">5 events that happened on 18 May, from 1291 to 1980.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1291</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mamluks capture Acre, the last great crusader city</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Sultan al-Ashraf Khalil's army stormed Acre after a six-week siege. The remaining crusader towns on the coast were abandoned within months, ending the crusader states in the Levant.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1804</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">French Senate proclaims Napoleon Emperor of the French</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A decree of the Senate made First Consul Napoleon Bonaparte hereditary emperor. He crowned himself in Notre-Dame in December, in the presence of Pope Pius VII.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1860</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Republicans nominate Abraham Lincoln for president</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At the Chicago convention Lincoln overtook the favourite, William Seward, on the third ballot. His election in November prompted Southern states to secede.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1896</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Plessy v. Ferguson upholds 'separate but equal'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The US Supreme Court ruled that racial segregation on Louisiana railways did not violate the Constitution. The decision underpinned Jim Crow laws until Brown v. Board of Education in 1954.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1980</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mount St Helens erupts in Washington State</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">An earthquake triggered a vast landslide and lateral blast that tore away the mountain's north face. Fifty-seven people died in the deadliest eruption in US history.</p></li></ol>
